# Registry entry tapd01 -- first testAsynPortDriver instance on the target.
# The instance name is the IOC's own name (iocBoot/ioctestAsynPortDriver) in
# the registry's charset: lowercase [a-z0-9-], which the dispatcher enforces
# because the same string becomes the systemd unit instance, the state
# directory and the procServ name, plus an ordinal for a second copy.
# Slot 1 (console 21010): slot 0 belongs to the fleet BLM IOC, and two
# instances on one slot cannot coexist -- the second one fails to bind its
# console port. EPICS assigns the default CA/PVA ports (5064/5075) because no
# CA_PORT/PVA_PORT is pinned here; pin them (recommended: slot base + 3/4,
# i.e. 21013/21014) when clients need a fixed port.

IOC_INSTANCE_INDEX=1
IOC_APP_DIR=/opt/epics/iocs/asyn-scope-ioc
IOC_PATH=iocBoot/ioctestAsynPortDriver
IOC_APP_NAME=testAsynPortDriver
# A site assigns P to a machine, so it is free-form; this example spells it
# from the instance name so a PV says which instance serves it. R stays as
# upstream wrote it -- the device root inside the IOC, not an instance.
P=TAPD01:
R=scope1:
# Written out even though it is what the dispatcher would use anyway
# (/var/lib/epics-ioc/<instance>): this instance is copied by eye, and the key
# is one of EPICS_IOC_MACHINE_KEYS, the lines the class projects onto the card.
# This IOC writes nothing there -- an application that does names its
# subdirectories in IOC_STATE_DIRS.
IOC_STATE=/var/lib/epics-ioc/tapd01
